item.description:
Author(s): Sean Kazuo Isaacs — Under neoliberalism, capitalist accumulation has become increasingly dependent on finance, including the financialisation of social reproduction. The centrality of finance to the reproduction of capital has been linked to both the global spread of informal and precarious forms of labour and the reconfiguration of state institutions according to the interests of finance capital. While the recomposition of capital, the working class, and the state has occurred unevenly, these processes have everywhere made the global working class more reliant on debt to ensure their own social reproduction, and these debt relations have become increasingly tied to the reproduction of finance capital. This paper demonstrates how, over the last several decades, intensified financialisation – defined as interest bearing capital (
                    IBC
                    ) playing a key role in capitalist accumulation – has made both the reproduction of capital and the reproduction of the worker more dependent on finance, without displacing the pivotal role in accumulation played by value-producing wage labour.
